Spec: AI-Generated Remediation Playbooks

Purpose:
  Safety guardrails for any playbook the AI generates, regardless of event type.
  These rules apply to ALL generated remediation playbooks and cannot be overridden
  by the AI model. The AI has full freedom to handle unknown events but must stay
  within these boundaries.

Must:
  Target only the reported host using the event_host variable.
  Use FQCN (Fully Qualified Collection Names) for all modules.
  Prefer ansible.builtin modules unless the task specifically requires another collection.
  Include block/rescue for error handling on critical tasks.
  Add descriptive task names that explain the remediation action.
  Use become: true only for tasks that require privilege escalation.
  Be idempotent - repeated runs must produce the same result without side effects.
  Check current state before making changes (gather evidence first, act second).
  Report what changed at the end of the playbook with a summary debug task.
  Use ansible.builtin.service with state: reloaded when restarting is not required.
  Limit scope to the specific issue described in the event.

Must not:
  Delete user data, application data, or databases.
  Remove packages unless directly related to the remediation.
  Modify firewall rules or network configuration.
  Change SSH configuration or authentication settings.
  Modify user accounts, passwords, or sudo rules.
  Use shell or command modules when an equivalent Ansible module exists.
  Include hardcoded credentials, tokens, or secrets.
  Reboot the host without an explicit approval variable (allow_reboot: true).
  Stop services without a corresponding restart or recovery step.
  Make changes to hosts other than the reported event_host.
  Disable SELinux or AppArmor.
  Modify kernel parameters without explicit justification in the task name.
  Write to /root or home directories.
  Use rm -rf or recursive force-delete in any shell task.
  Download or execute scripts from the internet.

Forbids:
  ansible.builtin.reboot without conditional check for allow_reboot variable
  ansible.builtin.raw module in generated playbooks
  shell tasks containing rm -rf or find -delete without path restrictions
  selinux module with state=disabled
  Tasks with no_log: false that expose sensitive data
